  </table>

  <p><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> Superscript, Numbers, and Bubble Text are your safest choices for Discord messages and status. For usernames and server nicknames, stick with superscript numbers — they have universal support across all Discord clients.</p>

  <h2>Discord-Specific Rendering Quirks</h2>
  <p>Discord handles text differently from browsers or social apps. Four things trip people up:</p>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Dark mode is the default:</strong> Over 90% of Discord users browse in dark mode. In dark mode, small text — especially Superscript and Subscript — can appear slightly dimmer or thinner than regular text. Superscript remains the most readable on dark backgrounds; Subscript nearly disappears</li>
    <li><strong>Mobile vs Desktop rendering:</strong> Small text appears slightly larger on Discord mobile (iOS/Android) than on desktop. A superscript message that looks perfectly readable on your phone may look too tiny on your friend's PC. Always test on both before setting a permanent nickname</li>
    <li><strong>Character limits are strict:</strong> Discord has a 2,000-character message limit and a 128-character custom status limit. Unicode small text characters count the same as regular characters — a superscript "a" is one character, just like a regular "a"</li>
    <li><strong>Server permissions override everything:</strong> Some servers restrict nickname changes or have character filters. If your small text nickname doesn't save, the server admin may have blocked Unicode characters — not something wrong with your text</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Where to Use Small Text on Discord</h2>

  <h3>Messages: Whisper with Tiny Text</h3>
  <p>Small text in messages adds a playful, understated tone. Discord users often interpret tiny text as "whispering" — it's a non-verbal cue that's become part of Discord culture. Use it for:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Inside jokes:</strong> A superscript reply to a friend feels like a private joke in a public channel</li>
    <li><strong>Quick status updates:</strong> "ᵇʳᵇ" (brb) or "ᵃᶠᵏ" (afk) in superscript is subtle and doesn't disrupt the conversation</li>
    <li><strong>Creative formatting:</strong> Combine small text with Discord's native markdown — *italics*, **bold**, ~~strikethrough~~ — for multi-layered formatting that no other messaging app can replicate</li>
    <li><strong>Announcements:</strong> A bold header followed by superscript body text creates visual hierarchy that makes server announcements easier to scan</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Server Nicknames: Create a Unique Identity</h3>
  <p>Your server nickname is how community members see you. Adding superscript numbers (e.g., "Phantom⁰⁷", "Luna²⁰²⁵") creates a unique identifier without looking like spam. Bubble Text nicknames like "ⓈⓉⒶⓇ" are eye-catching in member lists. Just check the server's nickname policy first — some communities restrict special characters.</p>

  <h3>Custom Status: Make "Online" Interesting</h3>
  <p>Discord's custom status feature supports Unicode text. Instead of the default "Playing Valorant" or "Listening to Spotify", try small text for a status like:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>"studying ʸᵒᵘᵗᵘᵇᵉ" (subtle self-deprecation)</li>
    <li>"𝐟𝐨𝐜𝐮𝐬 𝐦𝐨𝐝𝐞" in bold for a clean, intentional status</li>
    <li>"²⁰²⁶ ᵍᵒᵃˡˢ: ʳᵃⁿᵏᵘᵖ" — stylish new year resolution format</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Combining Small Text with Discord Markdown</h2>
  <p>One of Discord's strengths is Markdown — asterisks for italics, double asterisks for bold, and so on. Small text plays nicely with all of it. Examples:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Effect</th><th>Input</th><th>Output</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Bold + Superscript</td><td>**ʰᵉˡˡᵒ**</td><td><strong>bold superscript</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Italic + Bubble</td><td>*ⓗⓔⓛⓛⓞ*</td><td><em>italic bubble</em></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Spoiler + Bold</td><td>||**𝐬𝐞𝐜𝐫𝐞𝐭**||</td><td>hidden bold text</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Code block + Superscript</td><td>`ʰᵉˡˡᵒ`</td><td><code>inline code superscript</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>


  <h2>Small Text in Bots &amp; Webhooks</h2>
  <p>Discord bots and webhooks can send small text, but rendering depends on the bot's message intent. Bots using <strong>Rich Embeds</strong> strip Unicode styling from all embed fields — your small text will appear as regular text inside embed titles and descriptions. Bots using <strong>plain text messages</strong> (or webhooks with text-only payloads) preserve small text perfectly.</p>
  <p>If you're building a bot that sends small text messages, avoid Rich Embeds for small text content. Alternatively, put small text in the message body above or below the embed, not inside it. Webhook users: test your payload by sending to a private channel first — Discord's webhook parser handles Unicode differently between desktop and mobile.</p>

  <h2>Server Boosting &amp; Small Text: Custom Invites</h2>
  <p>Level 1+ boosted servers unlock <strong>custom invite backgrounds</strong> and <strong>animated server icons</strong>. While small text doesn't directly affect these features, you can use small text in the <strong>server invite splash text</strong> to create a polished, branded welcome experience. A superscript tagline above your rules or welcome message signals attention to detail.</p>
  <p>Server Discovery listings support Unicode text in the server description field. If your server is listed on Discovery, using small text creatively in your description can help your listing stand out in a sea of plain-text descriptions.</p>

